// The wood data of the game's environment destruction and damage model, game state only.
// Read by `src/trees.rs` with species.ron before play. Current HP and ram parameters are shared;
// `structure` holds required per-species material facts for the structural laws. No structural
// consumer reads these facts yet. The wall design owns the model narrative.
//
// `structure.<species>.along_grain_rupture_pa` (Pa): green-wood bending modulus of rupture,
// longitudinal extreme-fibre stress. The section strength law will compare it with bending
// stress at a trunk section. It is distinct from the perpendicular-to-grain timber seam in
// materials.ron, which building joints read.
// `along_grain_fracture_j_per_m2` (J/m²): mode-I opening work for tension ALONG the fibres,
// across a trunk section (LR/LT fracture systems). The blow law will spend it per separated
// section area. It is NOT splitting along the fibres (RL/TL), whose stress is across the grain.
// The saturated softwood analogue is explicitly ASSUMED for every shipped species; no matched
// green longitudinal-fracture measurements were found. It must not be called a measured tree
// material property. The cited FHWA table itself models its parallel values from correlations.
//
// `root_plate_overturning_nm_per_kg` (N m/kg): the root anchor strength law will read the
// critical overturning moment as this coefficient times FRESH STEM MASS (kg), with no extra g.
// This is an ultimate moment, not root separation work. The root rows describe unrotted trees
// on freely draining mineral soil, rooting depth 40-80 cm (MEASURED domain of Nicoll's A2 class).
// Applying that site profile to the game's trees is ASSUMED; it is not a coefficient for every
// soil. Rooting-depth classes and soil groups must be honoured by a future consumer. Nor may
// the current dry-timber substance density silently stand in for fresh stem mass: the root
// coefficient supplies no moisture or mass conversion. These facts still have no sim consumer.
//
// `substance` is the trunk's substance, a hard key of `assets/materials/materials.ron`.
// `hp_per_m2` is a trunk segment's HP pool per square metre of its cross-section at mid-height
// (the instance's, scale applied): a segment whose two end radii average r has
// `round(hp_per_m2 × π r²)` HP points, at least one. Each segment's pool takes the energy a round
// loses crossing that segment at the one energy-to-HP rate every hard pool shares
// (`assets/ballistics/energy_dose.ron`), and the segment whose pool empties is where the trunk
// breaks.
// `fell_k` (N·s/m², per m³ of dbh³) is the ram threshold's constant: a tick's
// `Σ impulse × lever ≥ fell_k × dbh³` fells the trunk.
//
// PROVENANCE: every number is authored `(value, Kind("source or reason"))`, Kind one of Measured,
// Derived, Tuned, Resolution, Unknown (`src/provenance.rs`); a value without one does not load. The
// loader refuses a non-positive one.
Wood(
    substance: "Wood",
    hp_per_m2: (1500.0, Tuned("provisional (issue 149), a round number calibrated loosely: the
        shipped fir's (fir_1) base segment, 0.405 m across at mid-height, holds 193 HP (DERIVED),
        so the 88 mm APCBC's centred hit there (156 HP, MEASURED) empties it at the second shot;
        shots to break every cell MEASURED in
        .agents/scratch/damage-model-2026-10-29/segment-pools-results.md")),
    fell_k: (9.0e5, Derived("ram-impulses.md 2026-10-26: a tick's Σ impulse × lever MEASURED in
        track_sandbox at 64 Hz, 6 substeps — sustained push 13.3 kN·s·m, 5 km/h ram 105–108,
        10 km/h 119–151, 20 km/h 248–303; at 9.0e5 N·s/m² a 0.50 m trunk (threshold 112 kN·s·m)
        holds a push and falls at 10 km/h, a 0.42 m one (67) falls at a 5 km/h bump. Measured on
        a spruce; one value for all wood")),
    structure: {
        "norway_spruce": StructuralWood(
            along_grain_rupture_pa: (36.0e6, Measured("MEASURED transcription: Merlin et al.,
                Large-scale modelling wind damage vulnerability (2025), Table 2, default Norway
                spruce MOR 36 MPa (green-wood ForestGALES parameter, not a fresh tree measurement).
                https://www.plantedforests.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/FECOS-D-25-00237_R1paper_only.pdf
                The table misprints its MOR unit; MPa follows the model definition, not kN/mm2.")),
            along_grain_fracture_j_per_m2: (22340.0, Tuned("ASSUMED saturated clear-softwood
                analogue, not measured Norway spruce: Murray, FHWA-HRT-04-097 (2007), section
                1.7.3, Table 10, saturated parallel mode I 2.234 MPa cm = 22340 J/m2 (DERIVED unit
                conversion). Source uses correlated model values for southern yellow pine and
                Douglas fir, no measured parallel-mode data. Species and living-trunk transfer
                are unverified. https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/safety/04097/04097.pdf")),
            root_plate_overturning_nm_per_kg: (154.6, Measured("MEASURED transcription of
                a field-data regression: Nicoll (2006), The effects of soil, terrain and wind
                climate on tree root system development and anchorage, Table 2-6 (printed p. 38),
                row NS, soil A, root class 2: Creg 154.6 N m/kg fresh stem mass; 22 trees at 2 sites,
                s.e. 19.2 N m/kg. Species codes in Table 2-3, soil/depth in Table 2-1: Norway spruce,
                freely draining mineral soil, medium rooting depth 40-80 cm. Rot and snapped trees
                excluded. Moment includes applied pull and overhanging self-weight; multiply by
                fresh stem mass in kg, not weight in N. Transferred site profile ASSUMED for the
                game, not universal soil anchorage. https://era.ed.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/bf65a702-ade0-4a94-8a60-e1eaf7e8b79c/content")),
        ),
        "scots_pine": StructuralWood(
            along_grain_rupture_pa: (46.0e6, Measured("MEASURED transcription: Merlin et al.
                (2025), Table 2, default Scots pine MOR 46 MPa, the green-wood ForestGALES
                parameter. Same source and MOR unit correction as norway_spruce above.")),
            along_grain_fracture_j_per_m2: (22340.0, Tuned("ASSUMED saturated clear-softwood
                analogue, not measured Scots pine: Murray, FHWA-HRT-04-097 (2007), section 1.7.3,
                Table 10, saturated parallel mode I 2.234 MPa cm = 22340 J/m2 (DERIVED conversion).
                Correlated model values for southern yellow pine and Douglas fir; species and
                living-trunk transfer unverified. Same FHWA source as norway_spruce above.")),
            root_plate_overturning_nm_per_kg: (146.4, Measured("MEASURED transcription of
                a field-data regression: Nicoll (2006), Table 2-6 (printed p. 38), row SP, soil A,
                root class 2: Creg 146.4 N m/kg fresh stem mass; 71 trees at 5 sites, s.e. 9.6 N m/kg.
                Tables 2-3 and 2-1 bind Scots pine, freely draining mineral soil and rooting depth
                40-80 cm. Rot and snapped trees excluded. Total applied-plus-self-weight moment;
                mass in kg, no extra g. Transferred site profile ASSUMED for the game. Same Nicoll
                thesis URL as norway_spruce above.")),
        ),
        "silver_birch": StructuralWood(
            along_grain_rupture_pa: (63.0e6, Tuned("ASSUMED species transfer of the MEASURED
                transcription in Merlin et al. (2025), Table 2: default birch MOR 63 MPa, the
                green-wood ForestGALES parameter. Table says birch, not specifically Betula
                pendula; treating it as silver birch is an analogue. Same source and MOR unit
                correction as norway_spruce above.")),
            along_grain_fracture_j_per_m2: (22340.0, Tuned("ASSUMED saturated clear-softwood
                analogue for hardwood, not measured silver birch: Murray, FHWA-HRT-04-097 (2007),
                section 1.7.3, Table 10, saturated parallel mode I 2.234 MPa cm = 22340 J/m2
                (DERIVED conversion). Correlated southern yellow pine/Douglas fir model value;
                neither species nor living-trunk transfer is verified. No suitable birch
                longitudinal-opening measurement found. Same FHWA source as norway_spruce above.")),
            root_plate_overturning_nm_per_kg: (146.4, Tuned("ASSUMED Scots-pine analogue
                for silver birch: Nicoll (2006), Table 2-6 SP/A2 coefficient 146.4 N m/kg fresh stem
                mass, on freely draining mineral soil with rooting depth 40-80 cm. The table has
                no birch row; no matched silver-birch fresh-stem-mass regression was read. This
                transfer is not a measured birch anchorage or a bound on it. Site profile also
                ASSUMED for the game; kg fresh stem mass, no extra g. Same Nicoll thesis URL as
                norway_spruce above.")),
        ),
    },
)
